\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Four importance measures converge (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$); Binoculars cross-perplexity features independently reach 78.1\% (merged: 82.6\%; Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation_bino}); four classifiers converge within 0.8\,pp (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}; Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}).
The accuracy ceiling resides in the feature space, not the classifier.
End-to-end classifiers lag by 14--33\,pp (d2 F1\,${\leq}$\,0.26; Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}, \ref{app:dcol_iterations}), likely because depth signal resides in passage-level distributional tail statistics that are not linearly accessible in pretrained representations (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}).
The strategy effect (9.3\,pp) exceeds the model effect (7.2\,pp), requiring decoding-conditioned deployment (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}); reference-model mismatch costs 4--11\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}), and a 7B self-reference probe reveals a scale ceiling ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$; Appendix~\ref{app:qwen7b_selfref}).
Accumulation and cross-boundary experiments (84.8\%; Appendix~\ref{app:accumulation_auc},~\ref{app:cross_boundary}) confirm process-level depth fingerprints.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Ordinal depth labels enable tiered curation, retaining $d{=}1$ text while filtering $d{\geq}2$ where collapse compounds; the lightweight extraction (19 statistics, one forward pass) supports inline deployment with ${\leq}$7\% calibrated false-positive rate (Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).
Recursive generation thus leaves fingerprints resolvable into ordinal depth ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}3$ in 8/9 cells); open questions include scaling beyond 1.5B, paraphrase robustness, and downstream curation (Appendix~\ref{app:curation_poc}, \ref{app:downstream}).

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods across all 9 cells.
Four classifiers (RF, XGBoost, ordinal multilayer perceptron (MLP), prototypical) converge within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), which confirms a feature-determined accuracy ceiling.

\paragraph{Neural end-to-end baselines.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (LoRA, 53--61\%) and RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal contrastive, 64.5\%) lag RF by 14--33\,pp; note that RF features access reference-model log-probabilities unavailable to end-to-end pretrained language model (PLM) baselines (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Perturbation-based (DetectGPT, 28.8\%) and membership-inference (Min-K\% Prob, 33.2\%) methods produce scores with no ordinal resolution (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}); Binoculars d1v2 AUC\,=\,0.50--0.53 vs.\ RF 0.93 (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}). Binoculars convergence with our pipeline is discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity (25.1\%) and zero-shot LLMs (best 36.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) confirm depth signal is inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method comparison (RF mean across 9 cells, 5-fold CV). Chance = 25\% / 33.3\% (d1--d3). $^\dagger$Adapted from binary detection; Binoculars 11-feat: $p{>}0.05$ vs.\ 19-feat RF (paired permutation test).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Calibration & 4-Cl (\%) & d1--d3 (\%) \\
\midrule
